WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.000
LINDSAY: Good evening, everyone.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.339
Welcome here to the Council on Foreign Relations
tonight.

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:11.270
I am Jim Lindsay, senior vice president and
director of studies here at the Council.

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:17.730
I also want to welcome everybody who is joining
us via the Internet as we livestream tonight's

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.200
discussion.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.800
I think you're going to find it to be both
timely and important.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:28.150
It is my great pleasure as the director of
studies to introduce tonight's guests.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:30.290
They are both terrific talents.

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:37.660
I am very honored that I can call both of
them my friends, besides being colleagues.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:41.609
Let me begin with the gentleman to my immediate
right, Ray Takeyh, who is senior fellow for

00:00:41.609 --> 00:00:45.600
Middle Eastern studies here at the Council
on Foreign Relations.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:51.460
Ray has written two books on Iran, along with
numerous articles and op-eds.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:57.609
He has also written widely on political reform
in the Middle East, as well as on Islamist

00:00:57.609 --> 00:00:59.370
movements and parties.

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:05.920
In 2009, Ray served as a senior advisor on
Iran in the U.S. State Department.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:12.180
For those of you who watch C-SPAN, he's probably
very familiar, because he has testified before

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.630
the U.S. Congress more than 20 times.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:22.880
To Ray's right physically, not necessarily
in the other way, is Steve Simon.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.880
Steve is currently a visiting fellow at the
Dickey Center for International Understanding

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.140
at Dartmouth College.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:36.280
Steve has had a long and distinguished career
in the U.S. government working on Middle Eastern

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.899
and terrorism issues.

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:44.180
Besides holding a number of posts in the U.S.
State Department, he has served on the staff

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:51.340
of the National Security Council, under both
presidents Clinton and Obama, most recently

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:56.590
as senior director for Middle East and North
African affairs.

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:02.630
Steve has also been a senior fellow at RAND,
at the International Institute for Strategic

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:06.360
Studies, and here at CFR.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:10.000
Like Ray, Steve has written widely and well
about a range of Middle East issues.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:16.030
And I believe, come fall, is going to be joining
the faculty at Amherst College in Amherst,

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:17.030
Massachusetts.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:21.680
Now, I'd be happy to talk about all the great
things that Steve and Ray have written in

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.920
the past, but our purpose here tonight is
to recognize the publication of their terrific

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.300
new book.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:34.410
I'll hold it up right now, "The Pragmatic
Superpower: Winning the Cold War in the Middle

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:35.410
East."

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:41.300
I say it's a terrific book and I'm not the
only one who has said this.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:47.730
Publishers Weekly said the authors "puncture
entrenched myths," "lucid and accessible."

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:53.500
The New York Times Book Review says it's an
"insightful, comprehensive account."

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:59.450
And Kirkus Review says, "It's a singular take
on U.S. foreign policy in the Middle East."

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:05.670
So please join me in welcoming Ray and Steve
tonight.

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:07.790
(Applause.)

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:10.840
Let me congratulate both of you on the publication
of the book.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:18.290
I know it's always nice to be done, and particularly
done with revisions.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:22.700
When you read "The Pragmatic Superpower,"
the takes readers on a historical journey

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:29.140
that begins with the Iran crisis of 1946,
goes all the way up through the 1991 Gulf

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:35.820
War, with stops along the way for the Suez
Crisis, the Six-Day War, and the Iranian Revolution,

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.580
among other major events.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:41.240
But let me ask-direct this toward you, Ray.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:47.430
Your assessment of U.S. policy during this
time is more positive than most historians'

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:48.700
take on this.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.260
What have previous writers gotten wrong?

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:59.460
TAKEYH: Well, I think they looked at the issue
perhaps, obviously, differently than we did.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:07.400
What we came to look at was how narrowly the
United States defines its interests.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:12.980
Not what those interests should have included,
but how were those interests defined.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.840
And they were defined narrowly, but not modestly.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:23.960
They were narrow in terms of access to oil,
security and stability of Israel, and finally

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:28.400
preventing Soviet Union from making inroads
in the region.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.470
You can make a case that these were not-and
others have-these are not sufficient set of

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:37.760
standards that United States should have and
others, but within the parameters of how United

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:44.570
States defined its interests it was rather
surprisingly capable of discharging those

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:51.970
across administrations and across vast changes
in the region and the global politics as well.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:57.600
So there was a sort of unusual consensus that
prevailed among different American administrations.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:02.790
And I think what we've suggested is those
interests were broadly successful.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.120
Not always.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:07.040
We don't make the case that United States
was successful at every instance.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.940
But you know, at the end of the day the balance
sheet kind of worked out correctly.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:16.070
LINDSAY: So, Steve, when was the United States
successful?

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:25.070
SIMON: Well, I think it was successful in
'48.

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:27.620
It was successful '73.

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:31.780
LINDSAY: Unpack. '48 would be the recognition
of Israel?

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:40.040
SIMON: There was-in 1948 the U.S. backed the
partition of Palestine into Arab and Jewish

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:42.200
states.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:49.820
And the administration recognized the state
of Israel over against the strong opposition

00:05:49.820 --> 00:06:02.010
of a bureaucracy that tended to misread Soviet
motivations on the one hand, and overestimate

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:07.590
the sensitivity of Arab governments on the
other, particularly the Saudis whose interests

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:14.260
were actually going to be dictated by their
threat perceptions and not their views of

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:15.260
Israel.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:26.340
In '73, the U.S., I think, was remarkably
successful in exploiting a devastating war

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:38.000
that took place in October 1973, to essentially
flip Egypt from the Soviet camp to the American

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:50.000
camp, and ultimately shepherd the results
of that very adroit diplomacy toward a peace

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.760
agreement between Egypt and Israel that, I
would say, decisively ended the prospect of

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:59.690
a major land war in the Levant.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:04.070
So, you know, two examples, you know, right
there.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:05.760
'67 less so.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:09.960
There was a war in 1967 between Arabs and
Israelis.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:15.490
It didn't work out quite that well because
of the extent to which the Johnson administration

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:21.830
was so preoccupied and distracted by the war
in Southeast Asia.

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:31.510
And that was an especially interesting episode
to study, because you could see in the course

00:07:31.510 --> 00:07:41.010
of events moments when U.S. intervention might
have obviated the war, and therefore have

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:45.889
produced what, in effect, would be a very
different Levant today.

00:07:45.889 --> 00:07:47.400
So that would be a counter example.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:48.900
LINDSAY: So what were the low points?

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:55.650
TAKEYH: The low points were usually occasions
where the United States not only had the false

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.870
assumptions, but failed to adjust them.

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:04.110
The first term of Eisenhower, with over investment
in Abdel Nasser as a vehicle for promotion

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:07.590
of the American interest, and pretty much
every day of the Carter administration.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:08.590
(Laughter.)

00:08:08.590 --> 00:08:10.430
LINDSAY: So you're tough on the Carter administration?

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:11.430
OK.

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:17.040
What did the Carter administration get wrong,
and why did it get it wrong?

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:18.040
TAKEYH: All right.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:21.690
Well, Steve can talk about the Camp David
portion of it, which they-actually they came

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:26.220
in with a different perception of how the
Palestinian-Egyptian-Israeli thing had to

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:31.280
work out, and they were preempted by Sadat's
visit to Jerusalem.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:37.519
On Iran, which was the big issue that they
got wrong, they had-there was a failure to

00:08:37.519 --> 00:08:41.589
understand the coming of the revolution, even
though there were some indications to that

00:08:41.589 --> 00:08:42.620
effect.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:49.379
And then failure to appreciate that revolutionary
situations don't always produce post-revolutionary

00:08:49.379 --> 00:08:50.379
moderates.

00:08:50.379 --> 00:08:53.660
And that's-
LINDSAY: You get more French Revolutions than

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:54.660
American Revolutions.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:55.709
TAKEYH: Almost always.

00:08:55.709 --> 00:09:01.300
LINDSAY: But do I take from reading your book
that you think that the Carter administration

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:03.759
could have helped the Shah remain in power?

00:09:03.759 --> 00:09:09.959
TAKEYH: Well, yeah, there's some new documentation
that has come to light recently, as has been

00:09:09.959 --> 00:09:12.649
published by BBC.

00:09:12.649 --> 00:09:20.360
The question is, could the United States have
done more to instigate a military intervention

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:22.970
to forestall the revolution?

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.980
We kind of make the case-
LINDSAY: Military intervention by whom?

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:26.980
TAKEYH: The Iranian armed forces.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:34.420
That in absence of shah and his collapse was
looking for some indications of support before

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:35.829
they'd take such bold action.

00:09:35.829 --> 00:09:37.490
And that was not forthcoming.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:42.300
I think that's what the missed opportunity
was, potentially.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:46.370
LINDSAY: Ray has concluded that each and every
day of the Clinton administration-

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:47.370
TAKEYH: Carter.

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:50.100
LINDSAY: Excuse me-the Carter administration
was an unsuccessful day.

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:55.839
But most people would, putting aside the Iranian
hostage situation, look at Camp David as the

00:09:55.839 --> 00:09:58.269
great success.

00:09:58.269 --> 00:10:03.170
SIMON: You know, it's really a tough-it's
really a tough call.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:09.589
I mean, I think, you know, from Ray's perspective-and
at this point, I feel perfectly free to speak

00:10:09.589 --> 00:10:11.360
on his behalf.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:12.360
(Laughter.)

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:13.720
TAKEYH: I don't.

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:14.720
(Laughter.)

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:23.380
SIMON: You know, in terms of strategic import
the fate of Iran and its relationship with

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:35.910
the United States far outweighed the strategic
import of the Camp David Accords.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:38.629
I'm sympathetic to that view, I think.

00:10:38.629 --> 00:10:46.170
I mean, by the time the Camp David Accords
were signed, the prospect of a war between

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:56.970
Israel and Egypt I think was fundamentally
distanced from any conceivable reality.

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:02.160
And I think it was also unlikely at that point
that the Soviets could have leveraged the

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:12.850
collapse of the Camp David talks-so even the
non-existence of a Camp David process-to lure

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:14.910
Egypt back into the Soviet camp.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:23.279
I think Sadat was at that point pretty firmly
ensconced on the American side of the fence.

00:11:23.279 --> 00:11:33.310
So if you-if you take those perceptions as,
you know, relatively accurate, then, you know,

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:41.689
I think you're going to look at the attention
that Carter lavished on the Camp David process,

00:11:41.689 --> 00:11:49.019
while Iran-while the situation in Iran was
deteriorating, as being fundamentally misplaced.

00:11:49.019 --> 00:11:58.720
Now, you know, this may have been due to Carter's
preoccupation with, you know, scriptural narrative

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:06.220
and, I think, you know, I guess a conviction
of a personal mission to bring Arabs and Jews

00:12:06.220 --> 00:12:15.449
together-what the Israeli defense minister
accused Secretary Kerry of, Messianism.

00:12:15.449 --> 00:12:23.779
But I do think at the end of the day the lesser
strategic goal was attained at the expense

00:12:23.779 --> 00:12:26.550
of the larger strategic interest.

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:28.290
LINDSAY: OK.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:33.499
Your book is about the Cold War, but the two
words I haven't heard either of you mention

00:12:33.499 --> 00:12:38.579
so far, Soviet Union, because obviously the
Cold War is a confrontation between the United

00:12:38.579 --> 00:12:39.999
States and the Soviet Union.

00:12:39.999 --> 00:12:45.399
And one of the objectives you talk about at
some length in the book is that U.S. policy

00:12:45.399 --> 00:12:50.970
toward the Middle East, toward this region,
was designed to prevent the Soviet Union from

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:55.569
establishing dominance in the region.

00:12:55.569 --> 00:13:00.889
In your sort of evaluation of or assessment
of the period you chalk it up as the United

00:13:00.889 --> 00:13:04.790
States succeeding in accomplishing that very
goal.

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:09.369
But along the way, as you write, and let me
just quote a sentence here: The United States

00:13:09.369 --> 00:13:14.470
prevailed because the Soviet Union faced even
more insurmountable barriers to its political

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:15.470
objectives.

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:21.540
And you write later that the Middle East was
more of a priority for the United States than

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:22.970
for Russia.

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:30.051
So is it the case that the United States won
the battle in the Middle East or the Soviet

00:13:30.051 --> 00:13:33.870
Union lost it, they never even fought it?

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:34.870
Ray?

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:35.999
TAKEYH: I'll start.

00:13:35.999 --> 00:13:41.749
The Soviet Union proved even more maladroit
than the United States.

00:13:41.749 --> 00:13:44.649
And that was a great advantage.

00:13:44.649 --> 00:13:51.329
For a variety of reasons, what the Soviets
offered, which at the end of the day was bad

00:13:51.329 --> 00:13:58.079
advice and shoddy arms, it wasn't like East
Asia or even Latin America.

00:13:58.079 --> 00:14:02.939
That fusion of nationalism and communism didn't
exist.

00:14:02.939 --> 00:14:08.749
Most of the political actors in the Middle
East at this time were more opportunists than

00:14:08.749 --> 00:14:10.619
ideologues.

00:14:10.619 --> 00:14:12.749
And so they would switch sides.

00:14:12.749 --> 00:14:16.699
The Soviet Union never developed a client
that was reliable.

00:14:16.699 --> 00:14:20.049
They never developed an alliance system that
was sustainable.

00:14:20.049 --> 00:14:24.329
Their relationship with the countries were
usually transactional.

00:14:24.329 --> 00:14:29.410
And in that particular sense, the United States
has advantages that the Soviets didn't.

00:14:29.410 --> 00:14:34.610
And one of them was that it had a relationship
with the strongest military power in the region,

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:35.970
the state of Israel.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:40.439
And eventually all the Arab states recognized
that if they want something from the Israelis

00:14:40.439 --> 00:14:47.670
in terms of territorial concessions or what
have you, the road had to go through Washington.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:52.079
By suggesting that the Soviet Union was more
maladroit, that doesn't mean they didn't invest

00:14:52.079 --> 00:14:53.589
heavily in the Middle East.

00:14:53.589 --> 00:14:57.600
In Egypt I think at some point was either
the second or third largest recipient of Soviet

00:14:57.600 --> 00:14:58.600
aid.

00:14:58.600 --> 00:14:59.600
SIMON: Yeah.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:00.600
And Syria, too.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:01.600
TAKEYH: And Syria, too.

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:02.600
They invested heavily.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:08.589
But they just couldn't develop a footprint
in the region similar to what they had at

00:15:08.589 --> 00:15:12.399
various stages in East Asia or Latin-certainly
in Eastern Europe.

00:15:12.399 --> 00:15:16.579
LINDSAY: Anything you want to add there, Steve?

00:15:16.579 --> 00:15:20.339
SIMON: We were, you know, lucky in our friends.

00:15:20.339 --> 00:15:22.319
TAKEYH: And our adversaries.

00:15:22.319 --> 00:15:25.939
SIMON: You know, the-
LINDSAY: So lesson number one: Pick the right

00:15:25.939 --> 00:15:26.939
adversaries.

00:15:26.939 --> 00:15:27.939
TAKEYH: Yeah.

00:15:27.939 --> 00:15:28.939
SIMON: Yeah.

00:15:28.939 --> 00:15:29.939
And pick the right friends.

00:15:29.939 --> 00:15:32.389
And you know, we were lucky in both respects.

00:15:32.389 --> 00:15:38.589
I mean, to some extent adversaries of the
U.S. were more like a loyal opposition, you

00:15:38.589 --> 00:15:40.910
know, than anything else.

00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:49.329
And you know, our friends had stability going
for them.

00:15:49.329 --> 00:15:59.019
In Syria you had multiple coups, leading up
to the ascendance of the Assad family there.

00:15:59.019 --> 00:16:00.869
It was extremely unstable.

00:16:00.869 --> 00:16:09.609
And when you're that unstable it's very difficult
to build and deploy an operationally effective

00:16:09.609 --> 00:16:17.279
army and combat, so you're at a disadvantage,
of course, there.

00:16:17.279 --> 00:16:25.790
And in the Gulf countries, till the British
left in 1971 on the Arab side of the Gulf

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:29.769
and Saudi Arabia, really quite stable.

00:16:29.769 --> 00:16:39.269
The most stable friend that the Soviets had
was Egypt, which is why it was so remarkably

00:16:39.269 --> 00:16:49.249
deft and decisive that Kissinger took Egypt
away from the Soviet Union.

00:16:49.249 --> 00:16:54.759
LINDSAY: And as you both know, there's a long
line of writing that argues that the U.S.

00:16:54.759 --> 00:17:00.929
relationship with Israel is a net drag on
U.S. policy in the region.

00:17:00.929 --> 00:17:03.569
I take it from your comments you disagree.

00:17:03.569 --> 00:17:10.100
TAKEYH: I can't think of an occasion when
that was the case during the period under

00:17:10.100 --> 00:17:15.939
consideration, an occasion where the Israeli-American
relationship was disadvantageous.

00:17:15.939 --> 00:17:18.630
It wasn't in '48.

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:19.890
Maybe in 1956.

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:27.579
Although, I would have to say, Israeli objectives
in 1956 were more thoughtful than French and

00:17:27.579 --> 00:17:28.579
the British.

00:17:28.579 --> 00:17:31.740
LINDSAY: But in 1956, the United States was
on the opposite side of the issue from Israel,

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:33.690
as well as from France and Great Britain.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:34.860
TAKEYH: Right.

00:17:34.860 --> 00:17:39.029
I can't think of an occasion where I would
say that the United States at this particular

00:17:39.029 --> 00:17:42.120
juncture was hampered by its ties with Israel.

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:46.010
Again, in some cases Israelis did the Americans
favors.

00:17:46.010 --> 00:17:49.500
In 1970 in Jordan, and what have you.

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:53.309
LINDSAY: Well, certainly in terms of, I mean,
Steve, you point about Americas friends.

00:17:53.309 --> 00:18:00.179
The relationship with Israel did not prevent
U.S. ties or relations with-certainly with

00:18:00.179 --> 00:18:01.179
the Gulf powers.

00:18:01.179 --> 00:18:02.179
SIMON: Well, no.

00:18:02.179 --> 00:18:06.070
I mean, I have-in answer to that, I really
have to go back to the question of threat

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:15.240
perceptions, which regulated Arab views toward
the U.S. relationship with Israel.

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:24.700
And when Arab threat perceptions were running
high-and that is to say perceptions of threats

00:18:24.700 --> 00:18:33.070
to their-vis-�-vis outside powers.

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:37.929
I'm not talking about internal, you know,
sort of domestic threats.

00:18:37.929 --> 00:18:43.420
But when they were afraid for their own security
they didn't worry too much about what the

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:46.250
United States was doing with Israel.

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:50.110
What they wanted was U.S. backing and support.

00:18:50.110 --> 00:18:58.539
And they were, I think, more or less comfortable
overlooking that other aspect of U.S. relations.

00:18:58.539 --> 00:19:00.030
TAKEYH: I'll say one more thing.

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:04.649
There was also-and Steve talked about threat
perception-there was also alignment of interests

00:19:04.649 --> 00:19:09.010
between Israelis and, at the very least, the
Gulf Arab States.

00:19:09.010 --> 00:19:12.700
And you saw that, for instance, in the Yemen
War-the first Yemen War.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:13.980
LINDSAY: Back in 1964?

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:16.399
TAKEYH: '62 to '64.

00:19:16.399 --> 00:19:19.750
LINDSAY: We were both very young then.

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:21.389
TAKEYH: Yeah.

00:19:21.389 --> 00:19:26.429
The Israelis do actually militarily participate
in that Yemen War.

00:19:26.429 --> 00:19:30.279
They run intelligence aircrafts.

00:19:30.279 --> 00:19:34.399
So there was always that undercurrent of alignment
between the Saudis and the Gulf States and

00:19:34.399 --> 00:19:35.620
the Israelis.

00:19:35.620 --> 00:19:40.410
It didn't always translate into policies that
they both could find attractive, but they

00:19:40.410 --> 00:19:46.320
shared a same kind of animosity toward rise
of revolutionary regimes that-mostly in the

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:48.090
Levant, but also in Iran.

00:19:48.090 --> 00:19:53.080
LINDSAY: Now, you argue in "The Pragmatic
Superpower" that policymakers could learn

00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:59.929
a lot today by looking back at a time when,
to use your phrase, hard-headed pragmatism

00:19:59.929 --> 00:20:06.580
helped the United States get things right
in the region.

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:13.350
What is it that today's policymakers have
forgotten or never learned?

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:14.350
Steve?

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:25.470
SIMON: Well, I think on the part of U.S. policymakers,
there's been an increasingly complex constellation

00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:32.880
of interests that the United States has tried
to maximize in the region.

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:40.520
And the more interests you're trying to maximize
simultaneously, and particularly when some

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:48.799
of those interests are not, at least on the
surface, compatible or reconcilable, then

00:20:48.799 --> 00:20:54.490
you can really wind up in a bit of trouble.

00:20:54.490 --> 00:21:08.100
Now, in the Cold War what we found was that,
as Ray said earlier, the U.S. was trying to

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:13.450
maximize a very small number of interests,
really.

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:16.279
Israeli security and access to oil.

00:21:16.279 --> 00:21:22.750
And both of these were linked in various ways,
and at different levels, with the need to

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:28.279
keep-the perceived need to keep the Soviets
out of the region, keep them off-balance and

00:21:28.279 --> 00:21:29.289
weak.

00:21:29.289 --> 00:21:33.529
And these were very well-defined issues.

00:21:33.529 --> 00:21:38.259
And they were seen as profoundly strategic
in Washington.

00:21:38.259 --> 00:21:43.650
And other interests-what we found was that
other interests simply weren't even considered.

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:46.830
LINDSAY: Hey can I draw you out on-can I draw
you out on what you think the other interests

00:21:46.830 --> 00:21:47.830
are in U.S. policy?

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:49.549
SIMON: I was going to get to that.

00:21:49.549 --> 00:21:57.500
So, for example, in the United States policymaking
world now, particularly in Democratic administrations

00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:03.760
but this was also true in Republican administrations
as well, especially the second Bush administration,

00:22:03.760 --> 00:22:12.639
there's a strong concern with democratization-for
example, political reform and liberalization,

00:22:12.639 --> 00:22:16.220
in the Arab world in particular.

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:27.570
This is an uneasy fit for the sort of more
traditional realpolitik interests that the

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:32.519
U.S. was seeking to maximize during the Cold
War.

00:22:32.519 --> 00:22:37.100
So, you know, the more complicated you make
things, you know, the more complicated they

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:38.100
get.

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:39.519
Now, you know, the world changes.

00:22:39.519 --> 00:22:43.760
So, you know, democratization just necessarily
became an interest.

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:44.809
And it was pursued.

00:22:44.809 --> 00:22:47.360
And you know, there it is.

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:50.320
But that simply wasn't a factor in the Cold
War.

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:56.809
Now, moving away from Washington to the region,
I'll just say that, you know, the elites in

00:22:56.809 --> 00:23:02.760
the region have changed in their perception
of the United States, in their perception

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:13.020
of their own interest, and in how they view
what was during the Cold War a fairly conventional

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:19.830
patron-client relationship, where depending
upon the circumstances the patron or the client,

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:22.759
you know, would hold-would hold the leverage.

00:23:22.759 --> 00:23:27.790
But I think it was-it was less complicated,
more straightforward, than now.

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:33.510
In the contemporary Middle East, you have
so many cross-cutting conflicts that I think

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:36.370
it would be difficult for any administration
to navigate them.

00:23:36.370 --> 00:23:42.640
And I'm not sure that they really existed
in the same complex way in the Cold War that

00:23:42.640 --> 00:23:43.640
they do now.

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:44.980
LINDSAY: So U.S. ambitions have changed.

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:48.360
The nature of the problem has changed.

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:51.990
So can hard-headed pragmatism work in this
environment?

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:57.370
TAKEYH: Well, let me just-the U.S. does always
have a reform agenda.

00:23:57.370 --> 00:24:00.580
But it's a reform agenda that says strong
states and strong leaders.

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:03.049
And it's focused on development.

00:24:03.049 --> 00:24:05.809
It's focused on fighting corruption.

00:24:05.809 --> 00:24:09.419
And you're dealing with states, at this point,
for instance, the Saudi state in the 1950s

00:24:09.419 --> 00:24:10.419
that was going bankrupt.

00:24:10.419 --> 00:24:14.720
It's amazing you can bankrupt Saudi Arabia,
but there they were.

00:24:14.720 --> 00:24:16.620
So it's-
LINDSAY: Well, oil was also at a much lower

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:17.620
price at that point.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:21.220
TAKEYH: Right, but getting them to develop
a cohesive treasury system, I mean, it was-that's

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:28.019
sort of the building blocks of a state operating
under various sort of ideas of modernization

00:24:28.019 --> 00:24:29.019
theory, and so on.

00:24:29.019 --> 00:24:34.690
So everybody kind of had the idea that these
governments should be able to better allocate

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:39.840
the resources, deal with their economies,
industrialization, diversification of economy,

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:44.390
and dealing with problems of peasantry as
it transitions to urbanization, and all those

00:24:44.390 --> 00:24:48.399
issues, which I think become less salient
as you get to the '90s and other where you

00:24:48.399 --> 00:24:54.059
kind of talk about the notion of democratization
and democratic reform, institutional reform,

00:24:54.059 --> 00:24:57.799
which becomes part of the Clinton administration
agenda, and certainly part of the post-9/11

00:24:57.799 --> 00:24:59.120
agenda.

00:24:59.120 --> 00:25:01.650
Now, the lessons.

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:07.340
Well, you're dealing with a Middle East that
to some extent has come unhinged.

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:12.000
The state system in the region is attenuated,
in some cases collapsed.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:21.720
You have failed states in Libya, Syria, Lebanon
always can fit that category, Yemen, Iraq.

00:25:21.720 --> 00:25:24.740
Your allies are less resilient.

00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:28.659
Your adversaries more ideological in terms
of their character.

00:25:28.659 --> 00:25:30.960
You have non-state actors, such as ISIS.

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:35.639
There's more of an ideological feel to it
than sort of the military officers who came

00:25:35.639 --> 00:25:38.690
to power wanting to stay in power.

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:43.840
And I think the lesson here is that for the
United States to get the same results that

00:25:43.840 --> 00:25:49.309
it did during the Cold War, it just has to
have the idea that it has to have more commitment

00:25:49.309 --> 00:25:50.840
of resources.

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:53.450
There has to be recognition-I was thinking
about this today-

00:25:53.450 --> 00:25:57.419
LINDSAY: But given the amount of money the
United-the blood and treasure-we spent in

00:25:57.419 --> 00:25:58.419
Iraq-
TAKEYH: Yeah, exactly.

00:25:58.419 --> 00:25:59.419
Exactly.

00:25:59.419 --> 00:26:00.659
LINDSAY: That would strike, I assume, many
people-

00:26:00.659 --> 00:26:01.659
TAKEYH: Right.

00:26:01.659 --> 00:26:07.890
And the answer to stability of Iraq and Afghanistan
is permanent American military forces that

00:26:07.890 --> 00:26:09.440
are not going to look like South Korea.

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:12.320
They are going to periodically engaged in
policing politics.

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:15.330
LINDSAY: Well, judging from public opinion
polls, that's not a particularly popular proposition.

00:26:15.330 --> 00:26:18.289
TAKEYH: Right, so this is-what does it require?

00:26:18.289 --> 00:26:20.470
This is the bill of fares.

00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:23.190
You want to pay it; you don't want to pay
it.

00:26:23.190 --> 00:26:28.059
Another thing that I think it would say-one
final thing-that you saw during the Cold War

00:26:28.059 --> 00:26:33.090
that perhaps you don't see today, although
you see it in a peculiar way, was a bipartisan

00:26:33.090 --> 00:26:38.799
consensus that the region mattered and we
had to succeed in it.

00:26:38.799 --> 00:26:43.470
I mean, that's-as you were suggesting-that's
under stress today because of public opinion,

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:48.490
because of the ideas of post-Iraq syndrome,
where there's a fatigue regarding straightening

00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:49.990
out the Arab politics.

00:26:49.990 --> 00:26:51.970
That didn't exist at that time.

00:26:51.970 --> 00:26:53.000
Everybody kind of was on the same page.

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:55.910
LINDSAY: So not only the region has changed,
but things have changed back here.

00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:56.910
TAKEYH: Yeah, right.

00:26:56.910 --> 00:27:00.490
LINDSAY: At this point I want to bring the
rest of the room into the conversation.

00:27:00.490 --> 00:27:04.970
Let me remind everybody that our meeting here
tonight is on the record.

00:27:04.970 --> 00:27:08.010
I will ask, if you want to ask questions raise
your hand.

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:15.789
And if I recognize you, I ask that you please
stand up, state your name and affiliation,

00:27:15.789 --> 00:27:21.340
and ask a question-hopefully a relatively
brief one, so we can get in as many questions

00:27:21.340 --> 00:27:22.880
as possible.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:24.210
TAKEYH: Or a long one.

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:25.210
(Laughter.)

00:27:25.210 --> 00:27:27.759
LINDSAY: Liz, right here in the front.

00:27:27.759 --> 00:27:34.059
Q: Liz Verville.

00:27:34.059 --> 00:27:36.840
What do you think we should have done in Iran?

00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:38.690
TAKEYH: During?

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:45.190
Q: Well, at the time that you said we were
too focus on the Camp David accords, and not

00:27:45.190 --> 00:27:46.450
enough on Iran.

00:27:46.450 --> 00:27:51.779
TAKEYH: I'll give you a long answer and the
short answer, and the short answer follows

00:27:51.779 --> 00:27:53.700
the long answer.

00:27:53.700 --> 00:27:55.399
(Laughter.)

00:27:55.399 --> 00:28:03.909
Starting in mid-1960s, and maybe that was
because of the preoccupation with Vietnam,

00:28:03.909 --> 00:28:11.279
the shah begins to move toward stifling parliamentary
system that Iran had, with strong elites and

00:28:11.279 --> 00:28:17.279
strong parliaments and strong politicians,
because he had an idea that you need a strong,

00:28:17.279 --> 00:28:18.749
decisive, autocratic leader.

00:28:18.749 --> 00:28:24.360
And a lot of people told him not to do that,
because then you kind of become the sole responsibility

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:26.929
for all the problems of the country.

00:28:26.929 --> 00:28:29.769
The Kennedy administration told him not to
do that.

00:28:29.769 --> 00:28:31.240
The Johnson administration also.

00:28:31.240 --> 00:28:38.389
So there's sort of a beginning of the rise
of a single-man rule in Iran, which was unusual

00:28:38.389 --> 00:28:42.710
for history of Iran and actually hasn't even
happened in the post-revolutionary Iran.

00:28:42.710 --> 00:28:46.080
So that-the rot is kind of deeper than that.

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:53.929
Once you get to the revolution, we made number
of suggestions or assertions.

00:28:53.929 --> 00:29:01.179
Number one, we don't subscribe to the notion
that the success of the revolution was inevitable.

00:29:01.179 --> 00:29:07.220
Two, we don't subscribe to the notion that
Iranian military evaporated.

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:10.590
The phrase that you often hear in literature
is melted like snow.

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:13.580
There's really no evidence there.

00:29:13.580 --> 00:29:20.580
Third, that the Iranian military was capable
of essentially restoring order had it been

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:26.360
properly guided, when the shah became mentally
incapable of doing so, by an outside act of

00:29:26.360 --> 00:29:29.320
the United States, in particular, could have
perhaps also.

00:29:29.320 --> 00:29:31.330
So those are the three things that we suggest.

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:35.940
Once you get to that brief revolutionary period
that really begins in August of 1978 and ends

00:29:35.940 --> 00:29:37.320
in February 1979.

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:41.000
This is not that long of a period.

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:45.080
So the long-term problems should have been
detected in the 1960s.

00:29:45.080 --> 00:29:50.240
And maybe the Iranian revolution in some strange,
ironic way, is the misbegotten offspring of

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:55.429
Vietnam War, when America was totally preoccupied
in one place.

00:29:55.429 --> 00:30:02.559
And then down the road, you have some missteps
by the Carter administration, along the lines

00:30:02.559 --> 00:30:03.800
that we suggest.

00:30:03.800 --> 00:30:05.800
LINDSAY: So the short answer is?

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:08.789
TAKEYH: You might have been able to shoot
your way out of it.

00:30:08.789 --> 00:30:09.789
(Laughter.)

00:30:09.789 --> 00:30:10.789
LINDSAY: OK.

00:30:10.789 --> 00:30:13.899
Well, but actually, on that, it would seem
to me that at least part of your story is

00:30:13.899 --> 00:30:18.740
that the United States had opportunities for
itself to intervene in the Middle East and

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:23.090
throughout the Cold War for a variety of reasons,
partly because of Vietnam, partly because

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:28.400
of the fear of a confrontation with the Soviet
Union, the United States passed on that, something

00:30:28.400 --> 00:30:31.110
that's not true in the post-Cold War era.

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:35.980
TAKEYH: Well, the story of this book ends
with a massive military intervention, the

00:30:35.980 --> 00:30:37.149
1991 Gulf War.

00:30:37.149 --> 00:30:41.461
LINDSAY: At the very time when the Cold War
is actually fundamentally changing already.

00:30:41.461 --> 00:30:46.279
TAKEYH: When the Soviet-American relations
is advantageous to execution of the Cold War.

00:30:46.279 --> 00:30:51.870
It is a story of American success when the
most important military intervention was the

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:55.539
1958 intervention in Lebanon, where I don't
think there were any fatalities, compared

00:30:55.539 --> 00:31:01.250
to the East Asian theater, where you had-basically
the United States was at war from 1941 to

00:31:01.250 --> 00:31:04.320
1975, Korean War, Vietnam War.

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:09.279
And even Latin America, you had interventions
in Nicaragua, and you had interventions in

00:31:09.279 --> 00:31:10.769
Panama and so on.

00:31:10.769 --> 00:31:17.610
It's a story of success without inordinate
military intervention.

00:31:17.610 --> 00:31:20.070
That's the remarkable thing about it.

00:31:20.070 --> 00:31:23.840
LINDSAY: In the back, sir.

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:28.230
Q: Harry Blaney from the Center for International
Policy.

00:31:28.230 --> 00:31:35.450
It seems to me that the gap in what we are
discussing here is of a past that almost is

00:31:35.450 --> 00:31:40.000
been upended in a large totality.

00:31:40.000 --> 00:31:48.549
The other side is a today where almost all
the countries and the struggles and the things

00:31:48.549 --> 00:31:55.690
that are taking place on the-particularly
we didn't talk at all about the Shia-Sunni

00:31:55.690 --> 00:32:04.100
conflict that has erupted and the way it has
taken the strength and, in effect, created

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:08.399
in the whole region nothing but conflict and
upheavals.

00:32:08.399 --> 00:32:11.490
And the same thing is true for Saudi Arabia.

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:16.509
No longer the, quote, "rich" nation that it
once was, the oil being different, in a different

00:32:16.509 --> 00:32:17.509
way.

00:32:17.509 --> 00:32:21.990
I used to work in what Middle East oil is
about for one point.

00:32:21.990 --> 00:32:31.010
And it seems to me that we got it all wrong
most of the time, because we saw the Middle

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:32.659
East in that narrow prism.

00:32:32.659 --> 00:32:39.169
I like your phraseology about, you know, we
got the strategy right but the goal was wrong.

00:32:39.169 --> 00:32:43.559
And I think that-or, the short-term interests,
versus the goals.

00:32:43.559 --> 00:32:47.620
And the problem with today is we have a totally
different landscape.

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:53.190
And American policy is trying to respond to
that new landscape.

00:32:53.190 --> 00:33:01.010
And my question really is, what is-to quote
Lenin-what is to be done in this new landscape?

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:02.299
Do we have the same interests?

00:33:02.299 --> 00:33:05.240
Are we going to keep pursuing a dead past?

00:33:05.240 --> 00:33:11.240
Or are we going to try to see beyond the present,
going back to your phrase?

00:33:11.240 --> 00:33:16.570
Are we simply trying to put down only ISIS
and conflict, or do you see a larger vision?

00:33:16.570 --> 00:33:18.749
And I'd like to hear your response.

00:33:18.749 --> 00:33:21.440
LINDSAY: Want to go first, Steve?

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:31.309
SIMON: You know, I think, you know, the lesson
that we drew from our historical analysis,

00:33:31.309 --> 00:33:38.909
you know, especially through the lens of the
present and our own, you know, experiences

00:33:38.909 --> 00:33:50.480
in government, is that the United States did
well when it acted prudently and with an economy

00:33:50.480 --> 00:33:57.059
of force, and an economy of other things.

00:33:57.059 --> 00:34:09.560
And you know, subsequently as its-the complexity
of its objectives expanded, and its ambitions

00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:14.800
expanded, the U.S. did less well.

00:34:14.800 --> 00:34:20.620
And as we were discussing before, that was,
you know, true in part because the region

00:34:20.620 --> 00:34:29.770
itself had evolved in ways that made it a
very difficult landscape in which to pursue,

00:34:29.770 --> 00:34:32.290
you know, complicated objectives.

00:34:32.290 --> 00:34:41.150
And the U.S. had changed.

00:34:41.150 --> 00:34:50.990
And you know, in the book, you know, the chapter
on 1956 led me to, you know, think about,

00:34:50.990 --> 00:34:59.810
you know, the United States as the analogue
to Great Britain in 1956.

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:09.010
In 1956, Great Britain, in league with Israel
and France, attempted to seize the Suez Canal

00:35:09.010 --> 00:35:14.570
and overthrow Nasser.

00:35:14.570 --> 00:35:24.150
The U.S. role in that episode was quite interesting,
but that's not where I'm going with this somewhat

00:35:24.150 --> 00:35:25.730
meandering point.

00:35:25.730 --> 00:35:26.770
(Laughter.)

00:35:26.770 --> 00:35:36.420
Where I am going with it is that the defeat
of Britain's effort in '56 broke the back

00:35:36.420 --> 00:35:43.150
of British influence in the Middle East.

00:35:43.150 --> 00:35:49.900
And it did so irrevocably, I mean, decisively.

00:35:49.900 --> 00:35:59.000
As the London Times said when Anthony Eden,
who was the prime minister during '56 died-they

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:08.060
said that he was-he was the last British prime
minister to believe that Britain was a great

00:36:08.060 --> 00:36:13.650
power, and the first to confront a crisis
which proved she was not.

00:36:13.650 --> 00:36:21.020
I look at the Second Gulf War along those
lines.

00:36:21.020 --> 00:36:30.280
You know, the U.S. staked all its prestige
on what it believed to be an existential fight

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:32.730
in Iraq.

00:36:32.730 --> 00:36:34.200
And it was humbled.

00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:38.550
The United States lost really rather badly.

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:48.730
And in the process, U.S. credibility declined,
I think, dramatically, in combination with

00:36:48.730 --> 00:36:56.020
other developments, especially the advent
of Islam-or the reemergence, I should say-of

00:36:56.020 --> 00:37:00.870
Islam as a crucial factor in regional politics.

00:37:00.870 --> 00:37:13.200
So, you know, I think there were choices that
the United States made that affected its ability

00:37:13.200 --> 00:37:16.670
to influence events in the Middle East, that
reduced its effectuality.

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:25.560
But there were also changes in the Middle
East itself that would have pushed the United

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:30.800
States to some extent to the margin, no matter
what.

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:36.420
TAKEYH: I think sometimes when you're having
this conversation, as is almost inevitable

00:37:36.420 --> 00:37:43.230
with historical conversations and even with
personal memories, it is the good past versus

00:37:43.230 --> 00:37:46.860
the disorderly present.

00:37:46.860 --> 00:37:51.810
The Middle East during the Cold War was a
disorderly place.

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:59.420
Every decade of the Cold War had an Arab-Israel
war-'48, '56, '67, '73, '82.

00:37:59.420 --> 00:38:01.770
There was interstate war.

00:38:01.770 --> 00:38:05.080
The longest-there was devastating interstate
conflict in the Middle East, was the Iran-Iraq

00:38:05.080 --> 00:38:11.230
War, with a million casualties and substantial
use of chemical weapons.

00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:18.270
There was a war in Yemen between Egypt and
Saudi Arabia-Egypt directly, Saudi Arabia

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:21.440
through proxy.

00:38:21.440 --> 00:38:26.690
There was a civil war in Lebanon, which is,
I think, analogous to the Civil War in Syria

00:38:26.690 --> 00:38:28.100
today.

00:38:28.100 --> 00:38:35.590
There was a problem of all the stuff-institutional
decay, problematic leaderships, and so on.

00:38:35.590 --> 00:38:38.310
It was a chaotic, disorderly place.

00:38:38.310 --> 00:38:43.220
If you're sitting-having this conversation
in the 1970s, you wouldn't be saying, well,

00:38:43.220 --> 00:38:44.930
this is great.

00:38:44.930 --> 00:38:45.930
(Laughs.)

00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:49.910
Oil embargo of 1973, which destabilized the
global economy.

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:53.240
The rise of popular revolution in Iran in
1979.

00:38:53.240 --> 00:38:59.050
The emergence of terrorism and then the emergence
of Islamist terrorist, where the complexion

00:38:59.050 --> 00:39:00.230
of terrorism changed.

00:39:00.230 --> 00:39:04.980
This is not a happy place.

00:39:04.980 --> 00:39:10.570
And to kind of try to realize your interest
in that is uncanny in some ways.

00:39:10.570 --> 00:39:14.140
And this goes back to your first question:
Why do other people look at the Middle East

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:17.760
and see a different picture of success than
we do?

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:22.780
Because they look at sort of the credit-the
debit side of the ledger.

00:39:22.780 --> 00:39:28.950
And what we're trying to suggest is there's
a credit side to the ledger as well.

00:39:28.950 --> 00:39:30.920
So the problems of Middle East today are different.

00:39:30.920 --> 00:39:34.490
I mean, history doesn't move in a linear way.

00:39:34.490 --> 00:39:36.400
Your problems aren't exactly the same.

00:39:36.400 --> 00:39:42.010
But in some cases, they look more manageable
if you kind of think about them in some sense.

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:48.380
The critical question to explore is the does
it matter question.

00:39:48.380 --> 00:39:51.930
Does the region still matter?

00:39:51.930 --> 00:39:56.520
And you know, that's the debate that the country
has.

00:39:56.520 --> 00:40:01.030
Usually presidential elections would be the
time for such grand debates.

00:40:01.030 --> 00:40:03.260
Maybe that's not the case today.

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:08.100
But what are the American interests in the
Middle East today, with the complexion of

00:40:08.100 --> 00:40:10.460
the oil markets changing?

00:40:10.460 --> 00:40:11.770
Is it just the question of terrorism?

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:16.590
Is it a question of stability in the region
being important for stability of Europe-the

00:40:16.590 --> 00:40:18.070
migration crisis.

00:40:18.070 --> 00:40:21.150
There are profoundly compelling humanitarian
interests in the region.

00:40:21.150 --> 00:40:26.000
The Syrian Civil War perhaps doesn't have
the strategic implication of Lebanon.

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:29.070
So maybe Lebanon Civil War didn't have that
many strategic implications, but certainly-

00:40:29.070 --> 00:40:32.510
LINDSAY: But the death toll was nowhere near
what we're seeing in Syria, nor was the spillover.

00:40:32.510 --> 00:40:37.880
TAKEYH: Syria's civil war takes probably twice
as much as the same civil war today.

00:40:37.880 --> 00:40:44.530
So there's a compelling humanitarian argument
and everybody has to figure out do these mandate

00:40:44.530 --> 00:40:50.670
or merit the type of American intervention
that can tranquilize the region, because it

00:40:50.670 --> 00:40:52.670
would be quite a substantial commitment, I
think.

00:40:52.670 --> 00:40:56.870
LINDSAY: But Steven's argument earlier was
about narrowing the range of interests, as

00:40:56.870 --> 00:40:57.980
opposed to expanding it.

00:40:57.980 --> 00:40:59.770
TAKEYH: Well, I'm just saying-
LINDSAY: Would you narrow it?

00:40:59.770 --> 00:41:04.590
TAKEYH: Well, I'm just saying the problems
are somewhat different, and they-and given

00:41:04.590 --> 00:41:11.720
the absence of a reliable state structure
in the region, your commitment to ameliorate

00:41:11.720 --> 00:41:13.940
them are going to be more substantial.

00:41:13.940 --> 00:41:18.350
As I said, in Iraq you're talking about permanent
American forces.

00:41:18.350 --> 00:41:22.730
In Afghanistan, you're talking about-it's
been 15 years, it'll be another 150 years

00:41:22.730 --> 00:41:27.480
to keep Taliban from coming to power, or a
successor radical militia, or to maintain

00:41:27.480 --> 00:41:28.600
the cohesion of Iraq.

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:30.170
These are permanent.

00:41:30.170 --> 00:41:32.240
And we don't talk about them as permanent.

00:41:32.240 --> 00:41:36.900
You know, United States withdrawals, the withdrawal's
back up, the withdrawal's back down, because

00:41:36.900 --> 00:41:39.390
that's not a conversation that is taking place.

00:41:39.390 --> 00:41:42.340
LINDSAY: OK, we have time for one last question.

00:41:42.340 --> 00:41:48.520
Before I take it, I just want to remind everyone
that this meeting has been on the record.

00:41:48.520 --> 00:41:52.520
Also, Ray and Steve's book is on sale in the
back of the room.

00:41:52.520 --> 00:41:56.760
I encourage you to add it to your bookshelf.

00:41:56.760 --> 00:41:58.010
And you get to have the last question.

00:41:58.010 --> 00:42:00.020
SIMON: It's the ideal stocking stuffer.

00:42:00.020 --> 00:42:01.020
(Laughter.)

00:42:01.020 --> 00:42:02.240
Q: My name's Tara McKelvey.

00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:03.240
I work for the BBC.

00:42:03.240 --> 00:42:07.870
I wondered if you could tell me more about-you
said, Steve, about when America becomes ambitious

00:42:07.870 --> 00:42:10.750
it becomes harder to carry out good policies.

00:42:10.750 --> 00:42:14.230
I'm wondering if you can tell us about what
the Clinton-the new Clinton administration

00:42:14.230 --> 00:42:18.030
might look like in terms of its ambition,
expansionism.

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:24.170
SIMON: I actually don't know the answer to
that question.

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:25.410
Probably nobody does.

00:42:25.410 --> 00:42:33.630
Probably, you know, the candidate-presumptive
candidate herself doesn't quite know.

00:42:33.630 --> 00:42:43.820
I mean, you know, the new administration's
actions are going to be regulated by constraints.

00:42:43.820 --> 00:42:47.690
And where there are constraints, it will be
constrained.

00:42:47.690 --> 00:42:54.350
And where there are opportunities I think-you
know, her instincts are activist.

00:42:54.350 --> 00:42:56.660
And it's generally acknowledged.

00:42:56.660 --> 00:43:04.460
So I think she'd be inclined to perceive-to
seize opportunities if she perceived them,

00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:05.940
if they exist.

00:43:05.940 --> 00:43:07.024
LINDSAY: Where do you think there are opportunities?

00:43:07.024 --> 00:43:08.990
Do either of you see opportunities in the
region?

00:43:08.990 --> 00:43:13.140
SIMON: In the Middle East?

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:15.680
(Laughter.)

00:43:15.680 --> 00:43:18.310
As they would say in Paris.

00:43:18.310 --> 00:43:19.320
(Laughs.)

00:43:19.320 --> 00:43:27.900
Well, you know, I could conceive of, you know,
interesting things.

00:43:27.900 --> 00:43:37.480
But they would require, you know, changed
conditions and the cooperation of other countries,

00:43:37.480 --> 00:43:40.700
particularly Russia and Iran.

00:43:40.700 --> 00:43:43.340
And I'm thinking here about Syria.

00:43:43.340 --> 00:43:50.130
You know, the best outcome for Syria at this
point is one which produces some kind of relatively

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:58.640
stable burden sharing arrangement between
parties to the conflict.

00:43:58.640 --> 00:44:03.580
I doubt that the parties right now are terribly
interested in such a thing.

00:44:03.580 --> 00:44:13.330
And the outside parties, who do exercise a
fair amount of influence, direct and indirect,

00:44:13.330 --> 00:44:21.960
whether it's the Saudis, you know, or the
Qataris, or the Turks, or the Russians, the

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:24.830
Iranians, what have you.

00:44:24.830 --> 00:44:27.820
They're not-I don't think they're all there
yet.

00:44:27.820 --> 00:44:33.850
I mean, they've all reached the conclusion
that I've just stunned you with, you know,

00:44:33.850 --> 00:44:43.130
about the utility of a burden sharing-that
is to say a power sharing-arrangement in Damascus.

00:44:43.130 --> 00:44:48.990
You know, it would certainly be better than
the capital moving to Raqqa, which is the

00:44:48.990 --> 00:44:53.260
ISIS headquarters.

00:44:53.260 --> 00:44:59.060
So I think there's an opportunity there that's
diplomatic in nature.

00:44:59.060 --> 00:45:00.750
I don't think it's kinetic.

00:45:00.750 --> 00:45:06.490
I don't think that there's a role for U.S.
military forces in Syria, quite apart from

00:45:06.490 --> 00:45:11.680
the role that these forces are now playing
in defeating ISIS.

00:45:11.680 --> 00:45:18.940
And I think they are defeating ISIS, certainly
in Syria, in combination with, you know, strange

00:45:18.940 --> 00:45:23.210
bedfellows, like the Kurdish militias.

00:45:23.210 --> 00:45:30.240
But anyway, I don't see a role for the United
States as a combatant in the civil war.

00:45:30.240 --> 00:45:37.370
But the diplomacy will be effective only if
it's multi-lateralized among key outside players,

00:45:37.370 --> 00:45:41.200
and particularly the Russians and Iran.

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:47.340
Perhaps in a new administration if the Iranians
remain in compliance with the JCPOA, the nuclear

00:45:47.340 --> 00:45:55.590
agreement, and the Iranians and the Russians
begin to see that if there isn't some kind

00:45:55.590 --> 00:46:04.160
of political settlement they'll be stuck supporting
a hollowed-out and impossibly overtime, desperate

00:46:04.160 --> 00:46:11.220
regime, constantly under attack, then, you
know, they may-they may come around.

00:46:11.220 --> 00:46:17.960
And that's an opportunity that a new administration
may want to take advantage of.

00:46:17.960 --> 00:46:23.170
LINDSAY: I'll note the irony, Steve, that-and
your book argues that the Soviet Union essentially

00:46:23.170 --> 00:46:28.710
lost in the competition with the United States
in the Middle East during the Cold War, but

00:46:28.710 --> 00:46:35.990
Russia now has been able to insert itself
as a major player, perhaps as a spoiler, certainly

00:46:35.990 --> 00:46:37.120
when it comes to Syria.

00:46:37.120 --> 00:46:42.450
But I want to ask you, Ray, do you see any
opportunities perhaps on Iran or elsewhere?

00:46:42.450 --> 00:46:48.190
TAKEYH: My view on Syria, and Steve is more
informed of this, are informed by the Lebanon

00:46:48.190 --> 00:46:49.190
Civil War.

00:46:49.190 --> 00:46:53.260
And maybe that's the wrong prism to look at
Syria.

00:46:53.260 --> 00:46:58.480
At this point, all the actors that I can decipher
in Syria are playing for keeps.

00:46:58.480 --> 00:47:03.740
They're not looking for a diplomatic arrangement.

00:47:03.740 --> 00:47:08.450
And should there be a diplomatic arrangement-I
mean, along the way there were many diplomatic

00:47:08.450 --> 00:47:14.740
agreements on Lebanon-the Riyadh agreement,
the Cairo agreement, the this agreement, the

00:47:14.740 --> 00:47:15.740
that agreement.

00:47:15.740 --> 00:47:19.420
Ultimately, I think somebody would have to
enforce it.

00:47:19.420 --> 00:47:23.870
Paradoxically, in case of Lebanon, that was
the Syrian troops.

00:47:23.870 --> 00:47:24.870
(Laughs.)

00:47:24.870 --> 00:47:30.910
I have to think there has to be some kind
of an external actor enforcing whatever agreement

00:47:30.910 --> 00:47:34.780
comes after the parties exhaust themselves.

00:47:34.780 --> 00:47:38.470
I don't know who that actor is.

00:47:38.470 --> 00:47:44.190
And defeat of ISIS that I hear about-ISIS
is a symptom of Sunni grievances.

00:47:44.190 --> 00:47:47.200
If they disappear tomorrow, there will be
another one.

00:47:47.200 --> 00:47:53.770
So the collapse of the state system, the sectarian
polarization, the competition between the

00:47:53.770 --> 00:48:00.070
Iranians and the Saudis, have all produced
conditions that make agreements difficult

00:48:00.070 --> 00:48:05.690
and, if achieved, rather unenforceable without
some external actor.

00:48:05.690 --> 00:48:10.730
And I think that's probably the case in Syria
for a long time to come.

00:48:10.730 --> 00:48:12.640
I think there'll be diplomacy in Syria.

00:48:12.640 --> 00:48:15.490
I think there will be agreements, like there
were in Lebanon.

00:48:15.490 --> 00:48:20.430
But eventually somebody has to-the end of
the Cold War facilitated the end of the Lebanese

00:48:20.430 --> 00:48:26.940
Civil War, and then the Syrian enforcement
of the compacts between various local actors.

00:48:26.940 --> 00:48:29.880
Along the way, there always are temptations
to, like, partition it.

00:48:29.880 --> 00:48:35.400
Whenever Americans confront complicated, confessional
situation, and multiethnic society say, I've

00:48:35.400 --> 00:48:36.650
got an idea let's partition it.

00:48:36.650 --> 00:48:37.720
SIMON: I know.

00:48:37.720 --> 00:48:39.500
I'm from the South Bronx.

00:48:39.500 --> 00:48:41.230
TAKEYH: Yeah, exactly.

00:48:41.230 --> 00:48:42.230
(Laughter.)

00:48:42.230 --> 00:48:47.950
You know, and the problem with partitioning
Syria or Iraq is none of the local actors

00:48:47.950 --> 00:48:52.630
want that, because if you say we're going
to partition Syria and have a little Alawite

00:48:52.630 --> 00:48:57.460
country, you know, that essentially is an
Iranian beachhead that Israelis and the Saudis

00:48:57.460 --> 00:48:58.460
don't want.

00:48:58.460 --> 00:49:04.700
And one of the final things I will say that
I think we-here our pragmatism is at fault.

00:49:04.700 --> 00:49:08.920
Is when-and maybe Steve will say something
about this-when Iranians and the Russians

00:49:08.920 --> 00:49:14.510
look at Syria, they are invested in Assad.

00:49:14.510 --> 00:49:17.090
And we say, how about another Alawite general?

00:49:17.090 --> 00:49:19.270
They don't want another Alawite general.

00:49:19.270 --> 00:49:22.960
They want Assad.

00:49:22.960 --> 00:49:28.600
And that's what pragmatically we say to them,
what's the difference between Alawites, you

00:49:28.600 --> 00:49:29.600
know?

00:49:29.600 --> 00:49:32.420
You have another guy who is committed to your
interests, and so on.

00:49:32.420 --> 00:49:37.850
But the personage of Assad I think is more
central in Iranian factor-I don't know what

00:49:37.850 --> 00:49:38.850
you would on the Russians?

00:49:38.850 --> 00:49:43.050
LINDSAY: OK, well, I think that the takeaway
here is that we don't have any ready-made-we

00:49:43.050 --> 00:49:49.330
don't have any ready-made solutions for the
next administration, whoever it will be led

00:49:49.330 --> 00:49:50.330
by.

00:49:50.330 --> 00:49:52.010
TAKEYH: But we're very good at unpacking the
challenges.

00:49:52.010 --> 00:49:53.010
(Laughter.)

00:49:53.010 --> 00:49:54.010
LINDSAY: You are.

00:49:54.010 --> 00:49:57.550
And it's detailed at great length in your
book, "The Pragmatic Superpower."

00:49:57.550 --> 00:49:58.550
TAKEYH: Lots of challenges.

00:49:58.550 --> 00:49:59.550
LINDSAY: Available in the back.

00:49:59.550 --> 00:50:03.410
So the historical roots of the situation we
face today.

00:50:03.410 --> 00:50:04.410
Please join me in thanking both Ray and Steve.

00:50:04.410 --> 00:50:05.410
(Applause.)

00:50:05.410 --> 00:50:05.411
(END)

